L89@P1: statistical. Systems like AutoFeat (Ionescu et al., 2024) and
L90@P1: OpenFE (Zhang et al., 2023) provide a wide set of features
L91@P1: to fit the original data, but lack a broader perspective of
L92@P1: optimization, namely an optimization process that encompasses domain knowledge so as to make new features based
L93@P1: on logic. Newer approaches (Abhyankar et al., 2025; Nam
L94@P1: et al., 2024; Hollmann et al., 2023; Han et al., 2024; ouyang
L95@P1: et al., 2025) propose to use an LLM, driven by its parametirc
L96@P1: knowledge, to transform the raw data into new attributes.
L97@P1: The setups rely on feedback loops where LLMs propose
L98@P1: features, and a traditional ML model is trained whose characteristics are then used to improve the next iteration of
L99@P1: feature extraction. Han et. al. (Han et al., 2024), Hollmann
L100@P1: et. al. (Hollmann et al.), and Abhyankar et. al. (Abhyankar
L101@P1: et al., 2025) provide the LLMs with a Python environment,
L102@P1: thus utilizing the models’ code-writing capabilities to express data transformations. All established approaches use a
L103@P1: single LLM architecture (Nam et al., 2024; Hollmann et al.,
L104@P1: 2023; Han et al., 2024; Abhyankar et al., 2025), thus not
L105@P1: leveraging the potential in multi-agent collaboration (Tran
L106@P1: et al., 2025). Furthermore, Abhyankar et. al. (Abhyankar
L107@P1: et al., 2025) show the importance of domain knowledge
L108@P1: in their ablation study, yet they and most others rely fully
L109@P1: on the paramedic knowledge of the LLM and In-Context
L110@P1: Examples. On the contrary, modern methods of incorporating external knowledge, like Retrial-Augmented Generation (RAG), allow models to substantially extend their
L111@P1: knowledge. Lastly, all earlier literature (Nam et al., 2024;
L112@P1: Hollmann et al., 2023; Han et al., 2024; Abhyankar et al.,
L113@P1: 2025; ouyang et al., 2025) relies on simplistic, quantitative
L114@P1: rewards (e.g., model accuracy) as the sole feedback signal,
L115@P1: whereas Rogue One introduces a rich, qualitative assessment of feature quality, stability, and redundancy, which
L116@P1: LLM-Agents are shown to be capable of producing (Zheng
L117@P1: et al., 2025).
L118@P1: Ouyang and Wang (ouyang et al., 2025) very recently proposed FELA, the first Multi-Agent AutoFE architecture.
L119@P1: Although addressing the use of external knowledge and collaborative agents, it still relies on quantitative rewards, a
L120@P1: rigid information flow, and an unnecessary level of complexity. FELA was not included in our quantitative comparison
L121@P1: due to the lack of a publicly available implementation and
L122@P1: reporting of scores on established datasets. Our work differs
L123@P1: from FELA’s by employing a more flexible agentic design
L124@P1: and a richer, qualitative feedback mechanism, as opposed to
L125@P1: FELA’s reliance on quantitative rewards.
L126@P1: 3. Method
L127@P1: The feature extraction task is formulated as an optimization
L128@P1: problem where the goal is to discover the set of optimal features X
L129@P1: ∗ ∈ Rk×n consisting of n feature vectors x⃗∗ ∈ R
L130@P1: k
L131@P1: .
L132@P1: Each element of x⃗∗ is the result of applying the transformations h : R
L133@P1: m → R on the corresponding row-vector
L134@P1: ⃗x ∈ R
L135@P1: m from the raw data X ∈ Rm×n. We denote h(X )
L136@P1: as applying h(·) to all n row-vectors in X , thus resulting in
L137@P1: a column vector of optimal features. To simplify, we use
L138@P1: the following notation when applying all learned transformations:
L139@P1: X
L140@P1: ∗ = H(X ) = {h1(X ), h2(X ), . . . , hm∗ (X )} (1)
L141@P1: The optimization task can therefore be framed as:
