L163@P2: the set of transformations H that E when applied to a known
L164@P2: tabular dataset X . For instance, given raw data X with
L165@P2: columns age, blood pressure, and height, a transformation h1 from the set H could be the symbolic function
L166@P2: h1(⃗x) = ⃗xage × ⃗xblood pressure, while another transformation
L167@P2: h2 might be h2(⃗x) = ⃗xheight/⃗xage.
L168@P2: 3.1. System Overview
L169@P2: The framework consists of three agents—the Scientist
L170@P2: Agent, the Extractor Agent, and the Tester Agent —operating as a decentralized role-based Multi-Agent system(Tran
L171@P2: et al., 2025), as illustrated in Figure 1. All three agents
L172@P2: of Rogue One are designed to explore the raw data in an
L173@P2: open-ended manner, in contrast to FELA (ouyang et al.,
L174@P2: 2025), which limits the exploration-tasked Agent to only
L175@P2: the data schema, thereby not accounting for statistical properties within raw data. Also, Rogue One implements a
L176@P2: flooding strategy where tens of attributes are created each
L177@P2: iteration (17 features on average during the experiments
L178@P2: from Section 4), for then being pruned based on statistical
L179@P2: testing by the Tester Agent, in contrast to other AutoFE systems (ouyang et al., 2025; Hollmann et al.; Abhyankar et al.,
L180@P2: 2025) which focus on extracting only a few high-quality features. There are no strict pruning rules; however, the Tester
L181@P2: Agent is prompted to prune features that do not contribute
L182@P2: meaningfully to the prediction task in terms of predictive
L183@P2: power, redundancy, and robustness.
L184@P2: The operational cycle proceeds as follows for the i-th iteration:
L185@P2: 1. The Scientist Agent analyzes the cumulative results
L186@P2: from all i − 1 previous cycles, stored in a central Test
L187@P2: Pool, to formulate a high-level search strategy. This
L188@P2: strategy is articulated as a natural language directive
L189@P2: called the Focus Area.
L190@P2: 2. The Extractor Agent receives the Focus Area and
L191@P2: generates new candidate features by synthesizing and
L192@P2: executing Python code to express Hi. This process
L193@P2: produces the numerical matrix X
L194@P2: ∗
L195@P2: i
L196@P2: of new features and
L197@P2: a structured JSON file of Feature Explanations containing the generating code and a semantic description
L198@P2: for each feature. The new features and explanations are
L199@P2: appended to the Feature Pool, which stores features
L200@P2: and explanations from previous iterations. The feature matrix X
L201@P2: ∗
L202@P2: is produced by concatenating extracted
L203@P2: features from all iterations X
L204@P2: ∗ = {X ∗
L205@P2: 1
L206@P2: ; . . . ; X
L207@P2: ∗
L208@P2: i
L209@P2: }.
L210@P2: 3. Equipped with a Python environment, the Tester
L211@P2: Agent plans and executes tests to assess the quality of
L212@P2: X
L213@P2: ∗
L214@P2: , indirectly also assessing H. In doing so, the Agent
L215@P2: prunes redundant and low-impact features and generates the Feature Assessment. The predictor model f
L216@P2: ∗
L217@P2: is trained and evaluated on the pruned data to calculate
L218@P2: evaluation metrics using E(f
L219@P2: ∗
L220@P2: ). The feature assessment and evaluation metrics are stored in the Test Pool
L221@P2: used by the Scientist Agent for the upcoming iterations.
L222@P2: A knowledge access tool, based on a Single Agentic
L223@P2: Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) setup (Singh et al.,
L224@P2: 2025), is made available to all the Agents to allow access
L225@P2: to external knowledge. The RAG setup consists of an LLM
L226@P2: agent tasked to decompose the user query into sub-queries,
L227@P2: perform retrieval for each sub-query, and curate a summary
L228@P2: that is passed back to the user. The choice of external
L229@P2: knowledge source is a design choice for any implementation, but it helps the aforementioned agents by expanding
L230@P2: their expertise beyond their parametric knowledge. The
L231@P2: source is a curated database of domain literature for complex research-intensive or sensitive domains, like medicine,
L232@P2: or an open-ended web search for general domains.
L233@P2: 3.2. Scientist Agent
L234@P2: The Scientist Agent orchestrates the feature discovery process by setting the Focus Area for each iteration. Its primary
L235@P2: function is to intelligently guide the search towards promising regions of the feature space by evaluating and assessing
L236@P2: test results together with domain knowledge, thus learning
L237@P2: from previous mistakes and successes, and utilizing prior
L238@P2: knowledge to explain the feature relations.
L239@P2: The role of the Scientist Agent is to guide the long-term
L240@P2: strategies for the agent-network, balancing exploration vs
L241@P2: exploitation, and ensuring a knowledge-informed feature
L242@P2: exploration. The Scientist Agent, therefore, takes the role of
L243@P2: orchestration within the network, similar to a project manager. The agent starts assessing the results from previous
L244@P2: cycles stored in the Test Pool, combined with the definitions
L245@P2: for the current features. Using its reasoning capabilities, the
L246@P2: LLM analyzes these top-performing features to identify a
L247@P2: “common ground”—an underlying semantic or structural
L248@P2: pattern (e.g., features derived from vital signs within the
L249@P2: first 12 hours). It may query the knowledge access tool to
L250@P2: enrich this analysis with external domain knowledge. Based
