L282@P3: Figure 1. The Rogue One system architecture operates as a continuous iterative loop, organized into three core stages, each managed
L283@P3: by a specialized agent. The cycle commences with the Scientist Agent (blue background), which analyzes the Test Pool data from
L284@P3: prior iterations to define a new Focus Area. Subsequently, the Extractor Agent (green) leverages this Focus Area to generate new
L285@P3: candidate features, which are then appended to the central Feature Pool. Finally, the Tester Agent (yellow) orchestrates the evaluation and
L286@P3: pruning phase. It employs ML models to generate Performance Metrics and a Feature Assessment, which together update the Test Pool.
L287@P3: Concurrently, its Feature Pruning function refines the Feature Pool, completing the loop and preparing the system for the next iteration.
L288@P3: on this analysis, the agent determines the search scope:
L289@P3: • Exploitive scope: If a clear, promising pattern is identified, the Focus Area directs the Extractor Agent to
L290@P3: generate variations and more complex combinations
L291@P3: related to the particular pattern.
L292@P3: • Exploratory scope: If no strong pattern emerges or if
L293@P3: a particular data modality is underexplored, the Focus
L294@P3: Area directs the Extractor Agent to investigate a new
L295@P3: area of the data.
L296@P3: The agent is equipped with a scratch pad, which it is instructed to use extensively to avoid having important details
L297@P3: being lost within the model’s context. When done, the
L298@P3: model revisits its notes and forms the Focus Area, a text
L299@P3: string that serves as the instruction for the next cycle.
L300@P3: 3.3. Extractor Agent
L301@P3: The Extractor Agent is responsible for translating the strategic Focus Area into concrete, machine-executable code representing symbolic combinations of the raw data. It therefore takes on the role as the main workforce, not concerned
L302@P3: with long-term planning, but focused on doing an isolated
