L300@P3: 3.3. Extractor Agent
L301@P3: The Extractor Agent is responsible for translating the strategic Focus Area into concrete, machine-executable code representing symbolic combinations of the raw data. It therefore takes on the role as the main workforce, not concerned
L302@P3: with long-term planning, but focused on doing an isolated
L303@P3: task, namely the extraction work. It does so by first undergoing a discovery phase, leveraging a Python environment to
L304@P3: explore the raw feature set X (from simple commands like
L305@P3: df.head() and df.describe(), to complex multiline queries) to load relevant data schemas and statistical
L306@P3: properties into its context window. Equipped with a scratch
L307@P3: pad, it notes down useful insights from the discovery phase
L308@P3: for further use. In the second phase, known as the extraction
L309@P3: phase, the LLM provides pairs of a transformation h and
L310@P3: corresponding justifications for why h(X ) is rooted in the
L311@P3-4: 4Rogue One
L312@P4: focus area. Requiring a justification for the transformation
L313@P4: forces the model not to suggest arbitrary features, but rather
L314@P4: features that align with the focus area. When the agent finishes its work, it has produced the set of transformations
L315@P4: Hi and the corresponding symbolic features X
L316@P4: ∗
L317@P4: i = Hi(X ),
L318@P4: stored in the Feature Pool, which are passed to the Tester
L319@P4: Agent.
L320@P4: 3.4. Tester Agent
L321@P4: The Tester is a two-component module; the first being an
L322@P4: evaluation of the predictor model f
L323@P4: ∗
L324@P4: , and the second being
L325@P4: an assessment of the features X
L326@P4: ∗
L327@P4: carried out by the Tester
L328@P4: Agent. The Tester is tasked with being the critical thinker,
L329@P4: evaluating the result of the work carried out by the two other
L330@P4: Agents. It therefore takes the role of a lab assistant or similar
L331@P4: to a ”red team” from software development.
L332@P4: The evaluation performs a 5-fold validation using an XGBoost model as f. It then reports the performance metrics
L333@P4: produced by E, which serves as the global performance indicator for X
L334@P4: ∗
L335@P4: . The Tester Agent, equipped with a general
L336@P4: Python environment, inspects X
L337@P4: ∗
L338@P4: and the corresponding H
L339@P4: to get an overview of the kind of features to assess (temporal,
L340@P4: geometric, spatial,...). It then uses the external knowledge
L341@P4: source to find the most appropriate way of assessing the features with respect to feature importance (predictive power),
L342@P4: stability (resilience to noise), inter-feature relation (redundancy and correlations), and any other evaluation criterion
L343@P4: it deems necessary. This allows for a rich, detailed, and
L344@P4: human-readable feedback signal, in contrast to a primitive
L345@P4: signal set by previous methods like LLM-FE (Abhyankar
L346@P4: et al., 2025) and FELA (ouyang et al., 2025). As a part
L347@P4: of the testing, the Tester Agent also prunes X
L348@P4: ∗
L349@P4: , hence discarding features deemed redundant or with low predictive
L350@P4: power. This is an essential counter mechanism to the flooding strategy of the Extractor Agent to reduce the feature
L351@P4: count and to overcome the curse of dimensionality. During
L352@P4: its work, the agent notes down its findings in the scratch pad,
L353@P4: similar to the other Agents. When finished, the agent produces a comprehensive markdown document, known as the
L354@P4: Feature Assessment (See example in Appendix B), where
L355@P4: it structures its findings. The assessment does not serve as
L356@P4: a recommendation for further investigation, but rather an
L357@P4: overview of the current feature set. This is done to keep
L358@P4: a clear division of tasks between the Tester Agent and the
L359@P4: Scientist Agent. The performance metrics and the feature
L360@P4: assessment are passed on and appended as a new entry to
L361@P4: the Test Pool, completing the cycle.
L362@P4: 4. Experiment setup and results
L363@P4: Rogue One is implemented using the GPT-OSS-120B1
L364@P4: model(OpenAI et al., 2025) is used as the LLM for all
L365@P4: Agents without any fine-tuning. The prompting follows the
L366@P4: role-based collaboration strategy, allowing for explicit labor division and leveraging models’ own expertise. All
L367@P4: system prompts are listed in Appendix A. The knowledge sources used by the lookup agent, as shown in Figure 3, consist of relevant literature for the Extractor and
L368@P4: Tester Agents, accessed through dense vector retrieval using
L369@P4: the Qwen3-Embedding-4B2embedding model (Zhang
L370@P4: et al., 2025). For the Scientist Agent, the vector database
L371@P4: is swapped for a web search to account for the variety of
L372@P4: domains within the test data. Rogue One was run for 10
L373@P4: iterations on all datasets.
L374@P4: The quality of Rogue One is compared with other stateof-the-art models on various classification and regression
L375@P4: datasets. We build upon the data and scores reported by
L376@P4: Abhyankar et. al. (Abhyankar et al., 2025) to ensure consistent and fair comparisons. In addition, we test Rogue
L377@P4: One’s temporal capabilities by comparing with state-of-theart time series models on a small hand of multivariate time
L378@P4: series classification problems from the UEA dataset (Ruiz
L379@P4: et al., 2021). FELA (ouyang et al., 2025) is not included
L380@P4: in the comparison due to the lack of available code and
L381@P4: implementation details.
L382@P4: Rogue One is informed of the dataset structure by a threeline prompt: 1) The task type and data modality (ex., classification on tabular data). 2) The global goal of the task (ex.,
L383@P4: predict the sale value of a house). 3) A short description
L384@P4: of the raw data (ex., ”height: the number of floors in
L385@P4: the building”). This three-line prompt is integrated into all
L386@P4: Agents to provide a clear definition of the overall goal of
L387@P4: the system.
L388@P4: 4.1. Classification
L389@P4: Rogue One is tested on 19 classification datasets, varying
L390@P4: in complexity and size, covering a broad range of domains
L391@P4: like medical, physics, financial, and game-based. The mean
L392@P4: accuracy over a 5-fold cross-validation for Rogue One and
L393@P4: other state-of-the-art models is shown in Table 1.
L394@P4: 4.2. Regression
L395@P4: Similarly, for regression, we report the mean normalized
L396@P4: RMSE score over a 5-fold cross-validation for XGBoost
L397@P4: models trained on features from Rogue One and various
L398@P4: other AutoFE models. The results are shown in Table 2.
